          summary: "Not a unified model — frames in, text out, nothing flows back to the video. Filed here because it is the cleanest statement of a constraint unified models hit: the cache-side role (encode once, park it, stay out of the sequence) and the sequence-side role (decode step by step) are mutually exclusive, and MOSS is fast precisely because vision is <em>permanently</em> the condition. The problem it attacks: streaming video LLMs stop ingesting frames while writing a reply, so the earliest possible correction is one reply-length late — not that revision is impossible, but that it is untimely. The fix has two halves and the write-up insists on keeping them apart. <b>Architecture</b> puts visual K/V in a separate cache read by 8 of 40 gated cross-attention layers, so a frame arriving mid-sentence is an append the next decoding step already sees; it works because the vision cache is read-only from the text side, re-queried every step, and never goes stale (unlike a text KV cache after a prompt switch). <b>Policy</b> adds a <code>&lt;|silence|&gt;</code> token gated by a plain inference threshold τ=0.6 — no training-time loss weighting needed — plus a turning-point token that lets the model cut its own sentence. Neither half works alone: fix only the cache and the model sees the new frame and keeps talking anyway. The reading log works through the KV-cache comparison in detail, including the correction I had to make myself: text and frames share <em>one</em> position axis, and the real difference is that a frame costs <b>one</b> position instead of ~100, so the model's own sentence is not stretched apart by every insertion. Also covers the temporal-RoPE fix (stock Llama-3.2-Vision applies no positional encoding in cross-attention at all, leaving frames mutually orderless), the 836K data pipeline where the question is fixed and the answer is rewritten as the stream advances — with replies deliberately truncated on overflow, which is where the interrupt behaviour is actually learned — the four training stages, and Algorithm 1 in readable pseudocode. Honest limits: 5× TTFT and 2.7× decoding over Qwen2.5-VL-7B while being larger, but it loses on most standard benchmarks, τ / pooling stride / the 8-layer placement were never swept, and <b>there is no quantitative real-time evaluation at all</b> — their own argument for why is the sharpest thing in the paper: accuracy alone can be gamed by stalling until the event is unambiguous.",
