          link:    "https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07639",
          summary: "Fudan + Shanghai Innovation Institute, built on Llama-3.2-11B-Vision (~10.7B measured). Their taxonomy: offline (whole clip, one answer), streaming (replies mid-playback but stops ingesting frames while generating), real-time (keeps perceiving during the reply). Thesis: \"perception must not be blocked by generation.\" 40 layers, hidden 4096, 8 gated cross-attention layers at [3,8,13,18,23,28,33,38]; vision goes through ViT → 2D mean pool → one linear projector (7680→4096) and is exposed as side-channel K/V, never entering the autoregressive text sequence. Injection is h ← h + tanh(g)·CrossAttn(h,V), so vision starts at identity and does not damage the base LLM. Their main modelling change is temporal RoPE on both sides of cross-attention — stock Llama-3.2-Vision applies none, leaving frames mutually orderless — with all tokens of one frame sharing their &lt;|video|&gt; placeholder index, so one frame = one position and the signal is purely temporal (requires fixed resolution). Real-time behaviour is a &lt;|silence|&gt; token gated at inference by p ≥ 0.6 (they state no training-time weighting is needed) plus a turning-point token decoded as \"…\", inside a two-state event-driven loop where a frame or a question is enough to trigger a prediction, and both can arrive mid-reply. Data: hierarchical captions (video/event/action) → Phase A change-point detection, state-dependent instruction generation (right before the change, wrong after; time-varying attributes only), and a revise-or-Silence walk where silent segments are deliberately kept out of the response history → Phase B commits it onto a 1 fps clock with the question arriving in the first 80% of its segment, replies starting in the last third, and 20–60 tokens per second; on overflow the reply is truncated and a turning-point token inserted, which is where self-interruption is learned. 836K such samples. Four stages: 15M image-text with the LLM frozen, 1.8M for video temporality, 8M offline SFT, then real-time SFT as specialization (LR 1e-5 → 5e-6, grad accum 4). Speed on one H200 with 256 frames: TTFT 1.95s vs 9.94s and 38.4 vs 14.3 tok/s against Qwen2.5-VL-7B, while being the larger model — because visual tokens never pass through self-attention. Quality is behind Qwen2.5-VL-7B on most of 24 benchmarks (OCRBench 677 vs 864, MLVU −8.4, TempCompass −12.9) but ahead on VSI-Bench (+7.9), Video-Holmes (+6.3) and VisuLogic; they attribute the gap to data and scale rather than architecture, which is a claim not a result. Stated limits: no quantitative real-time evaluation at all (only three qualitative demos), τ / pooling stride / cross-attention layer placement / mixing ratio never swept, no RL — SFT \"can only imitate the pace baked into the synthetic data\". They argue real-time evaluation must score accuracy and timeliness jointly, splitting timeliness into system-level (TTFT, max FPS) and decision-level (event end → choosing to reply), because accuracy alone rewards stalling until the evidence is unambiguous. Future work: a decision-level latency benchmark and RL with R = acc − λ·delay.",
